 "excluded_candidates": ["Acute cholecystitis: less likely due to atypical presentation with hypotension and lack of fever", "Ectopic pregnancy: lower-tier possibility given OCP use but remains valid due to sexually active status", "Acute pancreatitis: atypical for RUQ localization", "Hepatic rupture: low probability but considered due to alcohol-related cirrhosis risk"], "alarm_features": ["Hypotension (80/40 mmHg) and tachycardia (110 bpm) suggesting hemodynamic compromise", "Severe RUQ tenderness with end-organ perfusion concerns", "Alcohol abuse and OCP use as systemic risk factors"]}, "branches": {"B1": {"id": "B1", "label": "Gynecologic Emergency with RUQ Involvement", "parent": "ROOT", "level": 1, "status": "expanded", "prior": 0.3654738559905073, "posterior": 0.5125174621276631, "danger": 0.8, "actionability": 0.0, "explanatory_coverage": 0.0, "expand_score": 0.5, "evidence_for": ["The combination of chronic alcohol use and oral contraceptives creates a synergistic thrombotic risk profile for Budd-Chiari syndrome, providing moderate evide…"], "evidence_against": ["RUQ localization is atypical for acute pancreatitis (B4), but the presence of RUQ pain alone still provides moderate evidence for pancreatitis due to non-speci…"], "unresolved_questions": [], "children": ["B1.1", "B1.2", "B1.3"], "closure_reason": "", "turn_cost_to_refine": 1.5, "diagnosis_commitment_gain": 0.7, "interrupt_relevance": 0.9, "level_role": "family", "classification_axis": "anatomy", "representative_diseases": []}, "B2": {"id": "B2", "label": "Hepatic Vascular Occlusion", "parent": "ROOT", "level": 1, "status": "expanded", "prior": 0.18898289583383704, "posterior": 0.07407185228876513, "danger": 0.9, "actionability": 0.0, "explanatory_coverage": 0.0, "expand_score": 0.5, "evidence_for": ["RUQ localization is atypical for acute pancreatitis (B4), but the presence of RUQ pain alone still provides moderate evidence for pancreatitis due to non-speci…"], "evidence_against": ["The absence of biliary colic and jaundice weakens the likelihood of choledocholithiasis (B3) while rapid weight loss is a non-specific finding. This provides w…"], "unresolved_questions": [], "children": ["B2.1", "B2.2", "B2.3"], "closure_reason": "", "turn_cost_to_refine": 2.0, "diagnosis_commitment_gain": 0.8, "interrupt_relevance": 1.0, "level_role": "family", "classification_axis": "anatomy", "representative_diseases": ["peliosis hepatis", "budd-chiari syndrome", "sinusoidal obstruction syndrome"]}, "B3": {"id": "B3", "label": "Biliary Tract Obstruction", "level": 1, "status": "parked", "posterior": 0.059, "danger": 0.6, "parent": "ROOT", "closure_reason": "", "evidence_against": []}, "B4": {"id": "B4", "label": "Acute Pancreatic Inflammation", "level": 1, "status": "parked", "posterior": 0.059, "danger": 0.5, "parent": "ROOT", "closure_reason": "", "evidence_against": []}, "B1.1": {"id": "B1.1", "label": "Ectopic Pregnancy with RUQ Referral", "level": 2, "status": "closed_for_now", "posterior": 0.0, "danger": 0.9, "parent": "B1", "closure_reason": "", "evidence_against": ["Absence of amenorrhea and vaginal bleeding provides strong evidence against ectopic pregnancy, despite hemodynamic instability and OCP use."]}, "B1.2": {"id": "B1.2", "label": "Ovarian Torsion with RUQ Referral", "level": 2, "status": "parked", "posterior": 0.512, "danger": 0.7, "parent": "B1", "closure_reason": "", "evidence_against": ["The absence of adnexal tenderness and nausea/vomiting provides weak evidence against ovarian torsion, though exercise-induced RUQ pain remains a supportive fin…"]}, "B1.3": {"id": "B1.3", "label": "Endometriosis-related Peritoneal Inflammation", "level": 2, "status": "closed_for_now", "posterior": 0.0, "danger": 0.3, "parent": "B1", "closure_reason": "", "evidence_against": ["Rapid weight loss alone is not a recognized feature of endometriosis-related peritoneal inflammation. This finding provides strong evidence against endometrios…"]}, "B2.1": {"id": "B2.1", "label": "Budd-Chiari Syndrome (Hepatic Vein Thrombosis)", "level": 2, "status": "parked", "posterior": 0.058, "danger": 0.9, "parent": "B2", "closure_reason": "", "evidence_against": ["The synergistic thrombotic risk profile with RUQ pain provides weak evidence against Budd-Chiari syndrome due to the absence of ascites, a typical finding in a…"]}, "B2.2": {"id": "B2.2", "label": "Peliosis Hepatis with Hemorrhagic Complications", "level": 2, "status": "closed_for_now", "posterior": 0.011, "danger": 0.7, "parent": "B2", "closure_reason": "", "evidence_against": ["Absence of jaundice and distension provides weak evidence against peliosis hepatis, which typically presents with hemorrhagic complications rather than acute R…"]}, "B2.3": {"id": "B2.3", "label": "Sinusoidal Obstruction Syndrome (Hepatic Veno-Occlusive Disease)", "level": 2, "status": "closed_for_now", "posterior": 0.005, "danger": 0.8, "parent": "B2", "closure_reason": "", "evidence_against": ["The absence of jaundice and liver enlargement is neutral for Budd-Chiari syndrome, as these findings are not definitive for or against the condition in this co…"]}}, "frontier": [], "other_mass": 0.0, "pending_results": [], "actions_taken": [{"t": 2, "type": "ANALYZE_VIGNETTE", "content": "Does the absence of abdominal distension or jaundice in the vignette significantly weaken peliosis hepatis as a potential cause of acute RUQ pain?", "summary": "Absence of jaundice and distension provides weak evidence against peliosis hepatis, which typically presents with hemorrhagic complications rather than acute RUQ pain."}, {"t": 2, "type": "ANALYZE_VIGNETTE", "content": "Are the absence of jaundice and the recent rapid weight loss (non-alcoholic) more consistent with Budd-Chiari syndrome than sinusoidal obstruction syndrome?", "summary": "Absence of jaundice and non-alcoholic weight loss provides weak evidence favoring Budd-Chiari syndrome over sinusoidal obstruction syndrome in this thrombosis risk profile."}, {"t": 3, "type": "ANALYZE_VIGNETTE", "content": "Does the patient's athletic status and exercise-induced RUQ pain strengthen the case for ovarian torsion despite the absence of adnexal exam findings?", "summary": "The patient's athletic status and exercise-induced RUQ pain provide weak evidence in favor of ovarian torsion, though the lack of adnexal exam findings reduces this support."}, {"t": 3, "type": "ANALYZE_VIGNETTE", "content": "Does the synergistic thrombotic risk profile (OCP + alcohol) with subacute weight loss and RUQ pain provide sufficient evidence for Budd-Chiari syndrome despite absent ascites?", "summary": "The synergistic thrombotic risk profile with RUQ pain provides weak evidence against Budd-Chiari syndrome due to the absence of ascites, a typical finding in advanced cases."}, {"t": 3, "type": "ANALYZE_VIGNETTE", "content": "Are the absence of adnexal tenderness and nausea/vomiting sufficient to rule out ovarian torsion as the source of acute RUQ pain?", "summary": "The absence of adnexal tenderness and nausea/vomiting provides weak evidence against ovarian torsion, though exercise-induced RUQ pain remains a supportive finding."}, {"t": 3, "type": "ANALYZE_VIGNETTE", "content": "Are the absence of jaundice and lack of liver enlargement inconsistent with Budd-Chiari syndrome as the explanation for hemodynamic instability and RUQ pain?", "summary": "The absence of jaundice and liver enlargement is neutral for Budd-Chiari syndrome, as these findings are not definitive for or against the condition in this context."}], "differential_history": [{"top": [["B1", 0.25], ["B2", 0.25], ["B3", 0.25]]}, {"top": [["B1", 0.365], ["B1.2", 0.357], ["B2", 0.189]]}, {"top": [["B1", 0.513], ["B1.2", 0.512], ["B2", 0.074]]}], "deliberation": {}, "interrupt": {"active": true, "reason": "Hypotension (80/40 mmHg) and tachycardia (110 bpm) with end-organ perfusion concerns meet hemodynamic instability criteria. High-risk differentials (B1.2 ovarian torsion, danger 0.7; B2.1 Budd-Chiari syndrome, danger 0.9) require urgent stabilization.", "required_actions": ["Secure IV access and initiate aggressive fluid resuscitation for hypotension", "Prepare for urgent imaging (e.g., ultrasound) to evaluate Budd-Chiari syndrome or acute abdomen", "Monitor for signs of decompensation (altered mental status, worsening hypotension)"]}, "termination": {"ready_to_stop": false, "termination_type": "continue", "reason": "Leading posterior (0.512) < 0.75 and dangerous alternative B1.2 (ovarian torsion, danger 0.7, posterior 0.512) remains unresolved. Emergency override requires stabilization actions but diagnostic refinement (urgent imaging) is still pending."}, "turn_budget_used": 3, "estimated_remaining_value": 0.65, "max_turn_budget": 5, "latest_action_type": "ANALYZE_VIGNETTE", "diagnosis_readiness_score": 0.5120862270519214, "benchmark_output_ready": false, "static_question": "Which of the following is the most likely the diagnosis?", "static_options": [{"id": "A", "description": "Ectopic implantation of a blastocyst"}, {"id": "B", "description": "Inflammation of the pancreas"}, {"id": "C", "description": "Obstruction of blood flow through the hepatic vein"}, {"id": "D", "description": "Obstruction of the common bile duct by radio-opaque stones"}, {"id": "E", "description": "Vascular ectasia within the liver"}], "seen_evidence_ids": [], "seen_evidence_phenotypes": "{'Abnormal liver physiology', 'abdominal pain', 'gas', 'acute pancreatitis', 'Intermittent jaundice', 'Budd-Chiari syndrome'}", "mode_policy": {"benchmark_purity": true, "allow_external_knowledge": true}, "answer_option_mapping": {}, "tool_use_log": [], "root_revision_needed": false, "max_tree_depth": 3, "reasoning_ledger": {"anchor": {"hypothesis": "B1", "t": 0, "posterior_at_anchor": 0.25}, "leader": {"branch_id": "B1.2", "label": "Ovarian Torsion with RUQ Referral", "posterior": 0.512, "leading_since_t": 0, "n_revisions": 0}, "leader_evidence": {"confirming": 2, "disconfirming": 2, "last_disconfirming_digest": "The absence of adnexal tenderness and nausea/vomiting provides weak evidence against ovarian torsion, though exercise-i…"}, "action_intents": [{"t": 2, "intent": "broaden"}, {"t": 2, "intent": "broaden"}, {"t": 3, "intent": "confirm"}, {"t": 3, "intent": "broaden"}, {"t": 3, "intent": "refute"}, {"t": 3, "intent": "broaden"}], "considered_alternatives": ["B3", "B4", "B2.1"]}, "discriminator_hints": "[Knowledge Layer: coverage=75%, source=both]\n\nBiliary Tract Obstruction vs Acute Pancreatic Inflammation:\n  Favours first:  2-3 toe syndactyly, abdominal distention, abnormal astrocyte morphology, abnormal brainstem mri signal intensity, abnormal epiphyseal ossification\n  Favours second: abnormality of the face, agenesis of lateral incisor, bilateral cleft lip, bilateral cleft palate, cleft soft palate\n\nBiliary Tract Obstruction vs Budd-Chiari Syndrome (Hepatic Vein Thrombosis):\n  Favours first:  2-3 toe syndactyly, abdominal distention, abnormal astrocyte morphology, abnormal brainstem mri signal intensity, abnormal epiphyseal ossification\n  Favours second: acute hepatic failure, ascites, budd-chiari syndrome, cholecystitis, cirrhosis\n\nAcute Pancreatic Inflammation vs Budd-Chiari Syndrome (Hepatic Vein Thrombosis):\n  Favours first:  abnormality of the face, agenesis of lateral incisor, autosomal dominant inheritance, bilateral cleft lip, bilateral cleft palate\n  Favours second: acute hepatic failure, ascites, autosomal recessive inheritance, budd-chiari syndrome, cholecystitis\n\nRelated to Biliary Tract Obstruction: bile duct disease, bile reflux, biliary tract neoplasm (disease)\n\nRelated to Acute Pancreatic Inflammation: disease of orbital part of eye adnexa, orbital cellulitis, orbital osteomyelitis\n\nRelated to Budd-Chiari Syndrome (Hepatic Vein Thrombosis): hepatic vascular disease\n\n[Indirect reasoning chains (PrimeKG 2-hop):]\n  A 23-year-old woman presents to the emergency department with severe abdominal pain → arthralgia → Biliary Tract Obstruction\n  A 23-year-old woman presents to the emergency department with severe abdominal pain → abdominal distention → Biliary Tract Obstruction\n  The pain has been dull and progressive → arthralgia → Biliary Tract Obstruction"}
